# Iterators, yield return, and Custom Sequences

Deferred execution is not magic reserved for LINQ. `yield return` gives you the same laziness in your own methods.

## An iterator method

```csharp
static IEnumerable<int> Fibonacci(int count)
{
    int a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        yield return a;              // hand one value back, then pause here
        (a, b) = (b, a + b);
    }
}

foreach (var n in Fibonacci(8)) Console.Write(n + " ");   // 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13
Console.WriteLine(Fibonacci(1000).Take(3).Sum());          // 1, only 3 values computed
```

The compiler rewrites that method into a state machine class implementing `IEnumerator<int>`. Nothing in the body runs until the first `MoveNext()`, and it stops exactly where the consumer stops asking.

## Why it matters: streaming a large file

```csharp
static IEnumerable<string> ErrorLines(string path)
{
    foreach (var line in File.ReadLines(path))
        if (line.Contains("ERROR"))
            yield return line;
}

var firstTen = ErrorLines("app.log").Take(10).ToList();
```

Memory used: one line at a time, regardless of whether the log is 2 KB or 20 GB. The equivalent `List<string>` version reads the whole file first.

## Implementing IEnumerable<T> on your own type

```csharp
public class Ring<T> : IEnumerable<T>
{
    private readonly T[] _items;
    private readonly int _start;

    public Ring(T[] items, int start) => (_items, _start) = (items, start);

    public IEnumerator<T> GetEnumerator()
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < _items.Length; i++)
            yield return _items[(_start + i) % _items.Length];
    }

    System.Collections.IEnumerator System.Collections.IEnumerable.GetEnumerator()
        => GetEnumerator();
}

var ring = new Ring<string>(new[] { "a", "b", "c", "d" }, start: 2);
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", ring));   // c,d,a,b
```

Any type with a `GetEnumerator()` works in `foreach` and gains every LINQ operator for free.

## Extension methods: adding your own operator

```csharp
public static class SeqExtensions
{
    public static IEnumerable<(T Item, int Index)> WithIndex<T>(this IEnumerable<T> source)
    {
        int i = 0;
        foreach (var item in source) yield return (item, i++);
    }
}

foreach (var (name, i) in new[] { "a", "b" }.WithIndex())
    Console.WriteLine($"{i}: {name}");
```

The `this` on the first parameter is the entire trick. That is exactly how `Where` and `Select` are defined in `System.Linq.Enumerable`.

> [!NOTE]
> An iterator method cannot use `ref`/`out` parameters, and any argument validation you write inside it is also deferred, so a bad argument surfaces at the first `MoveNext()` rather than at the call. The fix is a small non-iterator wrapper method that validates eagerly and then returns the private iterator.

# Async, await, and the Task Model

Async in .NET is about **not blocking a thread while waiting**. It is not about doing several things at once, that is parallelism, and it is a different tool.

## The shape of an async method

```csharp
using System.Net.Http;

static async Task<string> FetchTitleAsync(HttpClient http, string url)
{
    string html = await http.GetStringAsync(url);   // thread is released while waiting
    int start = html.IndexOf("<title>") + 7;
    int end = html.IndexOf("</title>");
    return start > 6 && end > start ? html[start..end] : "(no title)";
}

var http = new HttpClient();
Console.WriteLine(await FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://example.com"));
```

- `async` lets you use `await` inside and rewrites the method into a state machine.
- `await` suspends the method, returns the thread to the pool, and resumes when the awaited operation completes.
- The return type is `Task<T>` for a value, `Task` for nothing, `ValueTask<T>` for hot paths that usually complete synchronously.

## Sequential vs concurrent

```csharp
// Sequential: total time = sum of both
var a = await FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://example.com");
var b = await FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://example.org");

// Concurrent: total time = the slower of the two
var taskA = FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://example.com");
var taskB = FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://example.org");
var titles = await Task.WhenAll(taskA, taskB);

var firstDone = await Task.WhenAny(taskA, taskB);   // whichever finishes first
```

Calling an async method **starts** it. Awaiting it is when you wait for the result, so start everything you want to overlap first, then await.

## The rules that keep async from hurting

```csharp
// Bad: blocks a pool thread and can deadlock in some contexts
var result = FetchTitleAsync(http, url).Result;
someTask.Wait();

// Good: await all the way up, including Main
static async Task Main() => await RunAsync();
```

- **Async all the way.** `.Result` and `.Wait()` turn a non-blocking call into a blocking one and can deadlock.
- **`async void` only for event handlers.** Anywhere else, an exception inside it cannot be caught by the caller and takes down the process.
- **Do not wrap CPU work in `Task.Run` inside a server.** It does not save threads there, it just moves the work between pool threads.

## Exceptions across await

```csharp
try
{
    await FetchTitleAsync(http, "https://does-not-exist.invalid");
}
catch (HttpRequestException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"network error: {ex.Message}");
}
```

`await` rethrows the original exception, so `try`/`catch` reads exactly like synchronous code. With `Task.WhenAll`, awaiting rethrows only the *first* exception, inspect `task.Exception` (an `AggregateException`) when you need all of them.

> [!WARNING]
> An unawaited `Task` is a fire-and-forget operation whose exceptions vanish silently. If the compiler warns "because this call is not awaited, execution of the current method continues", that warning is almost always a real bug.

# Cancellation, Parallelism, and Async Streams

## Cancellation tokens

Every async API in .NET takes a `CancellationToken` as its last parameter. Pass it down; never swallow it.

```csharp
using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

static async Task<string> LoadAsync(HttpClient http, string url, CancellationToken ct)
{
    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();            // cheap early exit
    return await http.GetStringAsync(url, ct);    // flows into the socket read
}

try
{
    var body = await LoadAsync(http, "https://example.com", cts.Token);
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("timed out or cancelled");
}
```

`new CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` gives you a timeout for free, and `cts.Cancel()` lets a user cancel from a button or Ctrl+C. `CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(a, b)` combines two triggers into one token.

## Parallelism: CPU work, not IO waiting

```csharp
var numbers = Enumerable.Range(1, 10_000_000).ToArray();

var total = numbers.AsParallel().Where(n => n % 3 == 0).Sum(n => (long)n);

Parallel.ForEach(files, file => Process(file));

await Parallel.ForEachAsync(urls, cts.Token, async (url, ct) =>
{
    var body = await http.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
    Console.WriteLine($"{url}: {body.Length} bytes");
});
```

- `AsParallel()` (PLINQ) and `Parallel.ForEach` spread **CPU-bound** work across cores.
- `Parallel.ForEachAsync` is for IO-bound work with a concurrency limit (`MaxDegreeOfParallelism`), the right tool for "fetch 500 URLs, 10 at a time".
- Parallel does not mean faster: below a few thousand items the coordination overhead usually wins.

## Shared state needs protection

```csharp
int counter = 0;
Parallel.For(0, 100_000, _ => counter++);          // WRONG: lost updates
Console.WriteLine(counter);                         // some number < 100000

int safe = 0;
Parallel.For(0, 100_000, _ => Interlocked.Increment(ref safe));   // correct

var bag = new System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentBag<int>();  // thread-safe collection
```

`counter++` is read-modify-write, three operations, and two threads can interleave inside it. Use `Interlocked`, a `lock`, or a concurrent collection.

## Async streams

```csharp
static async IAsyncEnumerable<string> ReadLinesAsync(string path,
    [System.Runtime.CompilerServices.EnumeratorCancellation] CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    using var reader = new StreamReader(path);
    while (await reader.ReadLineAsync(ct) is { } line)
        yield return line;
}

await foreach (var line in ReadLinesAsync("app.log", cts.Token))
    Console.WriteLine(line);
```

`IAsyncEnumerable<T>` is `yield return` plus `await`: lazily produced values that each require IO. It is how you stream database rows or a chunked HTTP response without buffering the whole thing.

> [!NOTE]
> `lock` cannot be held across an `await`, the compiler rejects it, because the continuation may resume on a different thread. Use `SemaphoreSlim` with `await semaphore.WaitAsync(ct)` when you need mutual exclusion around asynchronous work.

# ASP.NET Core: The Pipeline and Minimal APIs

```bash
dotnet new web -o TaskApi    # minimal API template
cd TaskApi && dotnet run
```

## The host, the pipeline, and endpoints

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();      // service registration phase

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();                       // middleware pipeline, order matters
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

app.MapGet("/health", () => Results.Ok(new { status = "ok" }));
app.MapGet("/tasks/{id:int}", (int id) => Results.Ok(new { id, title = "Ship it" }));
app.MapPost("/tasks", (TaskDto dto) => Results.Created($"/tasks/{1}", dto));

app.Run();

record TaskDto(string Title, bool Done);
```

Two phases, always in this order:

1. **Configure services** on `builder.Services`, everything the app can resolve later.
2. **Build the pipeline** with `app.Use*`, then map endpoints, then `Run()`.

Middleware is a chain: each component can act on the request, call the next one, and then act on the response on the way back out. `UseAuthentication` before `UseAuthorization` is not stylistic, the second one needs the identity the first one established.

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    var started = DateTime.UtcNow;
    await next(context);                                  // downstream runs here
    var ms = (DateTime.UtcNow - started).TotalMilliseconds;
    Console.WriteLine($"{context.Request.Path} -> {context.Response.StatusCode} in {ms:0}ms");
});
```

## Model binding and results

```csharp
app.MapGet("/search", (string q, int page = 1) => Results.Ok(new { q, page }));
// GET /search?q=dotnet&page=2  -> parameters bound from the query string

app.MapPut("/tasks/{id:int}", async (int id, TaskDto dto, ITaskStore store, CancellationToken ct) =>
{
    var updated = await store.UpdateAsync(id, dto, ct);
    return updated is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(updated);
});
```

Parameters bind by convention: route values by name, simple types from the query string, complex types from the JSON body, and registered services from DI. `Results.Ok`, `NotFound`, `Created`, `BadRequest`, `NoContent`, and `Problem` produce the right status codes and bodies.

## Grouping and validation

```csharp
var tasks = app.MapGroup("/tasks").WithTags("Tasks");

tasks.MapGet("/", (ITaskStore s, CancellationToken ct) => s.AllAsync(ct));
tasks.MapDelete("/{id:int}", async (int id, ITaskStore s, CancellationToken ct)
    => await s.DeleteAsync(id, ct) ? Results.NoContent() : Results.NotFound());
```

`MapGroup` shares a prefix, plus filters and metadata, across a set of endpoints.

> [!WARNING]
> Middleware order is the source of a large share of ASP.NET Core bugs. Anything registered *after* `app.MapGet(...)` in the pipeline never sees requests that an endpoint has already handled, and CORS or authentication placed after routing will silently do nothing.

# Dependency Injection, Configuration, and EF Core

## Dependency injection is built in

```csharp
public interface ITaskStore
{
    Task<IReadOnlyList<TaskItem>> AllAsync(CancellationToken ct);
    Task<TaskItem> AddAsync(TaskItem item, CancellationToken ct);
}

builder.Services.AddScoped<ITaskStore, EfTaskStore>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IClock, SystemClock>();
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherClient>();
```

| Lifetime | One instance per | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `AddSingleton` | Application | Caches, clocks, stateless helpers |
| `AddScoped` | HTTP request | DbContext, repositories, unit of work |
| `AddTransient` | Resolution | Cheap, stateless, short-lived services |

Constructor injection is how a class asks for a dependency:

```csharp
public class EfTaskStore(AppDbContext db, ILogger<EfTaskStore> logger) : ITaskStore
{
    public async Task<IReadOnlyList<TaskItem>> AllAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("loading all tasks");
        return await db.Tasks.AsNoTracking().ToListAsync(ct);
    }

    public async Task<TaskItem> AddAsync(TaskItem item, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        db.Tasks.Add(item);
        await db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
        return item;
    }
}
```

> [!WARNING]
> Never inject a scoped service (like `DbContext`) into a singleton. The singleton captures the first request's instance and keeps using it forever, which produces disposed-object errors and cross-request data leaks. The container catches this at startup only when scope validation is enabled, which it is by default in Development.

## Configuration and options

```json
{
  "ConnectionStrings": { "Default": "Data Source=tasks.db" },
  "Notifications": { "Enabled": true, "RetryCount": 3 }
}
```

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<NotificationOptions>(
    builder.Configuration.GetSection("Notifications"));

public class Notifier(IOptions<NotificationOptions> options)
{
    private readonly NotificationOptions _opts = options.Value;
}
```

Sources layer in a fixed order, each overriding the last: `appsettings.json`, then `appsettings.{Environment}.json`, then user secrets in Development, then environment variables, then command-line arguments. That is why `ConnectionStrings__Default` as an environment variable overrides the file in production without a rebuild.

```bash
dotnet user-secrets init
dotnet user-secrets set "ConnectionStrings:Default" "Data Source=dev.db"
```

Secrets belong there or in environment variables, never in `appsettings.json`.

## EF Core in ten lines

```csharp
public class AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> options) : DbContext(options)
{
    public DbSet<TaskItem> Tasks => Set<TaskItem>();
}

builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o =>
    o.UseSqlite(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")));
```

```bash
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate
dotnet ef database update
```

Queries are LINQ, translated to SQL and executed on the server:

```csharp
var overdue = await db.Tasks
    .Where(t => !t.Done && t.Due < DateOnly.FromDateTime(DateTime.UtcNow))
    .OrderBy(t => t.Due)
    .Take(20)
    .AsNoTracking()
    .ToListAsync(ct);
```

`AsNoTracking()` skips the change tracker on read-only queries, a meaningful saving on large result sets. And beware the N+1: a `foreach` that touches a navigation property issues one query per row unless you `Include` it up front.
